Z||000018||Spektyr||04-21-2001||10:34 AM||||Run with a Cat but no muffler?  I could be way off here, but isn't the Cat the one that's notorious for getting clogged and restricting airflow?<P>I'd think if you want to get really aggressive without going to straight pipes all the way, you'd lose the cat and go to a high-flow performance muffler.  Those things are pretty mean sounding even with a cat.<P>I have my cat-bypass straight pipe on the way in the mail at this very moment.  Probably be a week or two before I get around to hacking the cat out, but with the better flowing exhaust, the low-temp thermo and fan switch that are riding with it and the new coil, wires and plugs I'll be getting, this car oughta wake up quite a bit.<P>Oh, and the expensive synthetic oil...<P>It's getting a hairdryer someday, might as well get it used to the luxury cuisine.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Au nrod||04-21-2001||11:27 AM||||ya, your right about the cats some time getting clogged up. but i've been running mine since '84 and no problems. <P>i was thinking of two other things though. one, with the cat still on the car it's still legal. and two, someone told me that the cat give the correct back pressure so the engine preforms better. i'm sure some of you great mechanics out the will comment on this.||||reg||1
